# License

Two different things are in this package, under two different terms.

## 1. Derived structured data: CC BY 4.0

Everything this project produced, the extracted structured fields, the canonical entity
vocabulary, entity/ontology mappings, all derived tables, all QA evidence and all
documentation, is released under the **Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International
License (CC BY 4.0)**.

Full text: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode

**You must attribute.** Any use, redistribution, or derivative work must credit:

> POTS/Dysautonomia Cross-Condition Literature Findings, machine-extracted structured
> findings from the POTS, dysautonomia and comorbid-condition literature.

**And you should credit the underlying researchers.** The scientific content summarized here
is the work of the source papers' authors. Every row carries `pmid`, `doi`, `title`,
`journal` and `year`. If a finding matters to your work, read and cite the original paper.
Attributing this database is not a substitute for that.

## 2. Verbatim quoted text: NOT licensed by us

The `provenance_quote` column contains **short verbatim excerpts from the source
publications**. That text is the **copyright of the respective publishers and authors**. It is
not ours to license, and CC BY 4.0 does **not** apply to it.

### Why the excerpts exist

Each excerpt is the span of source text an extracted finding was derived from. It is included
for one purpose: letting a reader **verify that the finding actually appears in the cited
paper**. This is the single most important quality property of the dataset, a finding without
its source span cannot be checked by anyone. Removing the column would make the corpus
unfalsifiable.

### What the excerpts actually are, measured, not asserted

Measured over all 148,186 rows of the full corpus:

| property | value |
|---|---|
| median length | 180 characters |
| 90th percentile | 298 characters |
| 99th percentile | 432 characters |
| under 400 characters | 98.2% of rows |
| over 600 characters | 97 rows (0.07%) |
| longest | 1,065 characters |

An excerpt is typically **one sentence** of a results or discussion paragraph. The
per-package figures are recomputed in `qa/package_stats.json` under `quote`, and
`code/verify_package.py` re-derives them from the shipped data.

### Constraints we place on ourselves, and ask you to keep

- Every excerpt is stored **adjacent to its full citation** (`pmid`, `doi`, `title`,
  `journal`, `year`) and never distributed detached from it.
- Excerpts are **single spans from a single location** in a paper, with no ordering or
  offset information retained that would allow reassembly.
- The corpus is a **distributed index across thousands of papers**, not a substitute for any
  individual article.

If you redistribute this data: keep the excerpt column with its citation columns, do not
extract it into a standalone quote corpus, and do not present excerpts without their
citations.

**Takedown:** if you are an author or publisher and want an excerpt removed, that is a
reasonable request and it will be honored.

### Amendment note (supersedes the Tier 1 v1 license text)

The Tier 1 v1 license constrained a column named `provenance_quote_trunc` to ≤200 characters.
That column belonged to the Tier 1 website export and does not exist in this corpus, whose
column is `provenance_quote` and was never truncated. This clause replaces that text and
describes the object actually shipped. The substantive commitments, excerpts travel with
their citations, no standalone quote corpus, honored takedown, are unchanged and now stated
about the real column. The ≤200-character figure was a self-imposed policy, not an external
requirement; it is superseded by the measured distribution above.

This is not legal advice, and the maintainer is not a lawyer. If your use requires a
determination on fair use, quotation exceptions, or text-and-data-mining provisions in your
jurisdiction, consult your institution's library or scholarly-communications office.

## No warranty

The data is machine-extracted and contains errors. It is provided as-is, with no warranty of
accuracy, completeness or fitness for any purpose.

**Scientific fidelity is unmeasured.** Every check in `qa/` verifies well-formedness, quotes
trace to their source, identifiers carry authority provenance, invariants hold. Nobody has
verified that the extracted science is *correctly interpreted*. That requires a human reader.

This is **not medical advice** and must not be used for diagnosis or treatment decisions.
